fips-initramfs (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release. Starts a FIPS node in the initramfs and binds
    dropbear to its fips0 address, for remote unlock of an encrypted
    root filesystem over the mesh.
  * The package carries the fips daemon and fipsctl, taken from the
    latest FIPS release at build time and verified against that
    release's checksums. Installing this one package is now the whole
    procedure for a machine that runs no FIPS daemon of its own;
    extracting the binaries out of the fips .deb by hand is gone. A
    machine that does install the fips package still uses /usr/bin
    first, so its initramfs node matches its daemon.
  * README.Debian says that a second install asks nothing and keeps the
    identity, and that seeing a first install means purging first.
  * The boot script reports both the name and the address: "FIPS node up
    at <npub>.fips (<address>)". The name is what a connection must use,
    and printing the address alone invited the one mistake the rest of
    the package warns against. The hook writes the npub into the image
    beside the address; an identity with no fips.pub is reported by
    address alone, as before.
  * Take the first address in the premount script with sed rather than
    head. Ubuntu's busybox-initramfs has no head applet and its image no
    head binary, so the parser returned empty on every call: the wait ran
    to its limit on every boot, the script reported that remote unlock
    was unavailable while it was working, and the address comparison it
    exists to make was never reached. Debian's image carries head, so
    this was invisible there. Found by installing on Ubuntu 24.04.
  * tests/premount-test.sh runs that parser with a PATH that has no head,
    which is the shape of the fault.
  * tests/hook-test.sh skips with a message when initramfs-tools is not
    installed, rather than failing three dozen assertions at one cause.
  * The install asks for the SSH public key allowed to unlock the machine
    and writes it to /etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys, which
    belongs to no package. It asks only when that file names no key, and
    never overwrites one. An answer that is not a public key is refused
    with an explanation rather than written.
  * An SSH session into the initramfs runs cryptroot-unlock and cannot
    open a shell. The forced command is added to the copy of
    authorized_keys inside the generated image, so the file in
    /etc/dropbear/initramfs stays untouched. Set
    FIPS_FORCE_UNLOCK_COMMAND=no to turn it off during bring-up.

 -- Johnathan Corgan <johnathan@corganlabs.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 07:35:06 +0000
